## Listen2, a text to speech reader for iPhone and iPad

Listen2 is a text to speech app for iPhone and iPad (iOS 18.2 or later) that reads documents aloud in natural voices and highlights each word as it is spoken. The voices run on the device itself, so it works with no internet connection, and documents are never uploaded to a server. It reads PDF, EPUB, DOCX, DAISY, Markdown and plain text files, text from the clipboard, and web articles shared from Safari, which it strips of ads and menus. For PDFs it reads multi-column pages in the right order, rejoins words split by hyphens, skips footnote markers, and recognizes the text of scanned pages on the device. It comes with {{ listen2Voices.voiceCount }} English voices from Listen2's own voice engine (American, British and Australian), which are installed with the app and work from the first launch. Free downloads add 25 English Phonon voices and 10 Supertonic voices, each of which reads 13 languages: English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, Portuguese, Russian, Turkish, Greek, Hindi, Vietnamese, Swedish and Hungarian. Listen2 works out which language a book is written in and reads it in that language, and in each of the 13 it reads numbers, dates and abbreviations the way a person would say them. Apple's system voices, including the Enhanced and Premium voices, can be used as well. The Listen2 Voices, new in version 2.0, come from an engine Moonquake Media trained from scratch on 7,410 hours of speech from 7,924 speakers; they run on the device with no network needed to speak, read English only, and each has its own Voice Profile with Pace (seven steps, plus Voice decides) and Liveliness settings. Listen2 Voices read heteronyms such as "read", "lead" and "tear" by their meaning in the sentence. They also take pronunciation instructions: a Sounds Like rule spells a word by its sounds in a phonetic respelling, with hyphens between the parts and the stressed part in capitals (Saoirse is SEER-shuh, Nguyen is nuh-WIN), and each spelling stands for one IPA phoneme, so the voice says exactly those sounds. The training text added modern writing from many subjects, among them medicine, animals, music, science and food, and modern company and brand names. In 2.0 a user can also make a voice shaped by their own reading of a short passage, on the device and without uploading anything; the person whose voice it is says a consent sentence aloud first, and Listen2 checks that the same person spoke both times. The made voice has the general character of the recording and is not an exact copy. Word-by-word highlighting works with the neural voices and not only with Apple's voices; the page scrolls along, and highlighting can be set to whole sentences instead. When a PDF or EPUB reaches a chart, diagram or photo, a model on the device can describe it aloud. Other features include pronunciation rules for names and terms, playback speed from 0.5x to 2.5x, background playback with lock screen and headphone controls, a sleep timer, bookmarks and search, collections that play several documents in order, optional iCloud sync between iPhone and iPad, and export of any document as plain text. It is fully usable with VoiceOver, with rotor navigation by sentence, line, paragraph and heading and Magic Tap for play and pause, and it offers the Atkinson Hyperlegible and OpenDyslexic reading fonts. It is used by blind and low-vision readers, people with dyslexia, students, commuters, and people who want to listen to books and articles without sending them to a cloud service. Listen2 has no account, no subscription, no ads and no tracking. It is free to try for 7 days and then a single purchase (US$24.99 as of November 2026) that covers both iPhone and iPad. An internet connection is needed only to download a voice, import a web page, or use iCloud sync. Listen2 is made by Zach Swift at Moonquake Media.

